[Themes "pin-on-c3,central-break"]
[For "black"]
[Kind "sideline"]
[Opponent "3.f3"]
[Start "short-system 2...d5"]

3. f3 e6 { Supports d5 solidly. The pawn on f3 takes the best square from White's king's knight, so White is behind in development. } 4. Nc3 ( 4. e5 c5 { Hits d4 at once. White's f3 pawn does nothing to guard it, and White's king's knight has lost its best square. } ) ( 4. Bd3 c5 { The same break: d4 is the pawn holding White's centre together. } ) ( 4. c4 dxe4 { Takes the pawn. If White recaptures with the f-pawn, ...e5 hits d4 and White's centre falls apart. } 5. Nc3 ( 5. fxe4 e5 ) 5... Bb4 ) ( 4. Be3 Qb6 { Hits b2, which lost its guard when the bishop left c1. } ) ( 4. c3 c5 ) ( 4. exd5 exd5 { Black has an open game and quick development, and White's f3 pawn is now just a weakness near the king. } ) 4... Bb4 { Pins the knight on c3 to the king and so weakens White's control of d5 and e4. } 5. Bd3 ( 5. Bd2 Ne7 ) ( 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 dxe4 { Opens the position while White's doubled pawns on c2 and c3 are slow and weak. } ) ( 5. Ne2 Ne7 ) ( 5. e5 c5 ) ( 5. Be3 Ne7 ) 5... Ne7 { Develops, and prepares ...dxe4 followed by ...Ng6 or ...O-O. If White plays a3, the bishop can take on c3 and ruin White's pawns. } 6. Nge2 dxe4 { Opens the centre while White's king is still in the middle and White is behind in development. } 7. fxe4 ( 7. Bxe4 O-O ) 7... e5 { Strikes at d4 before White finishes development. The central break gives Black an equal game. } 8. Be3 ( 8. a3 Ba5 9. Be3 Nd7 ) 8... exd4 { Opens the e-file against White's king in the middle and keeps the pieces active. } 9. Nxd4 O-O { King safe. The bishop on b4 and the knight on e7 are well placed. } 10. O-O Nd7 { Develops the last minor piece. The position is balanced. Black's plan: put a rook on the e-file, aim a knight at e5 and trade pieces. If White varies, castle and use the e-file. } *
